//dynamic programming is a method for solving complex problems by breaking them down into simpler subproblems, storing the results of those subproblems to avoid redundant work. It is often used to optimize recursive algorithms that have overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure properties.
// The main idea behind dynamic programming is to store the results of previously computed subproblems in a table (usually an array or a hash map) so that when the same subproblem arises again, we can simply look up the result instead of recomputing it. This technique can significantly reduce the time complexity of algorithms, often from exponential to polynomial time. 
// There are two main approaches to dynamic programming: top-down (memoization) and bottom-up (tabulation). In the top-down approach, we start with the original problem and recursively break it down into smaller subproblems, storing the results in a cache. In the bottom-up approach, we iteratively solve smaller subproblems first and use their results to build up the solution to the original problem.
// Dynamic programming is widely used in various fields such as computer science, operations research, economics, and bioinformatics to solve problems like the Fibonacci sequence, knapsack problem, longest common subsequence, and many others. It is a powerful technique that can lead to efficient algorithms for a wide range of problems.